# Git commit message format

## Overview

This rule defines **mechanical** commit layout only (subject shape, line width, body bullets). It keeps subjects consistent with branch/ticket naming and avoids mixing in unrelated formats unless the user opts in.

## When to Use

- The user asks for a commit message, subject line, or formatted commit text.
- You are proposing `git commit` wording after completing a change.
- You summarize a commit in chat (mirror the same shape and width).

**When not to use** (defer elsewhere)

- **What the message should argue or explain** — intent, context, risk: follow **`rules/ao-git-workflow-and-versioning-rule.mdc`**, especially **Descriptive Messages**.
- Repos or tasks where the user explicitly requests a **different** convention for that commit.

This file **supersedes** Conventional Commits examples (`feat:`, `fix:`) elsewhere when they conflict with **subject-line shape** and **72-column layout** here.

When the user **does** ask for a commit, use this **subject line** shape (one line, **72 characters or fewer** when practical):

```text
<TICKET> <TYPE>: <short imperative description>
```

## Parts

- **`<TICKET>`** — Issue id from context (e.g. `ATC-2363`). Prefer the branch name (`ATC-2363-FEATURE-…`) or what the user/Jira ticket states. If unknown, ask once instead of inventing a number.
- **`<TYPE>`** — Short uppercase category, e.g. `FEATURE`, `FIX`, `REFACTOR`, `MODEL`, `DEV ENV`, `INTERNAL`, `DOCS`, `TEST`. Match types already used on the branch when obvious.
- **`<description>`** — Imperative, specific; trailing period only if the team already uses full sentences there.

### Subject line examples

```text
ATC-2363 FEATURE: Activity History - Telemarketer Dropdown
ATC-2363 DEV ENV: Add telemarketer seeds
ATC-2363 REFACTOR: Extract quote activity history edit policy load
ATC-2363 MODEL: Add telemarketer associations on computed and fact models
```

**Do not** switch to unrelated formats (e.g. Conventional Commits `feat:` only) unless the user explicitly requests that repo’s alternate convention for this task.

## Line width

- **72 characters** maximum per line for the subject and for every body line (including wrapped continuation lines of a bullet).
- Wrap long lines at word boundaries when a line would exceed 72 characters.

## Body layout (optional)

- Use a **blank line** after the subject when there is a body.
- **Bullets** start with `- ` when the body is a list; wrap to 72 columns.

Whether and how much body to add is **content** — see **`rules/ao-git-workflow-and-versioning-rule.mdc`**.

When summarizing a commit in chat, mirror the same **shape** and **line width**.

## Format examples

### Subject-only

```text
ATC-2423 FIX: Null-safe quote lookup when policy number missing
```

## Common Rationalizations

| Rationalization | Reality |
|---|---|
| "I'll use `feat:` — it's standard." | Here, **`<TICKET> <TYPE>:`** wins unless the user asked for another convention for this task. |
| "I'll guess the ticket id." | **Ask once** if unknown; do not invent ids. |
| "Subject can run long." | Hard-wrap at **72 columns** (subject and body lines). |
| "I'll skip bullets and wrapping in chat summaries." | Chat summaries of commits should still match **shape** and **width**. |
| "Format is enough; no need for why." | Format does not replace **`ao-git-workflow-and-versioning`** on substance. |

## Red Flags

- Subject missing ticket or type token, or type not uppercase-style when this rule applies.
- Conventional Commits-only subject without user opt-in.
- Lines longer than 72 characters without word-boundary wrapping.
- Invented ticket numbers.

## Verification

After drafting or editing commit text under this rule, confirm:

- [ ] Subject matches `<TICKET> <TYPE>: <imperative description>` (or user-approved alternate for this task).
- [ ] No line exceeds **72 characters** (subject and body).
- [ ] Ticket sourced from branch/context/user; **no invented** id.
- [ ] Substance (why, risk, pointers) aligns with **`rules/ao-git-workflow-and-versioning-rule.mdc`** when a body is used.
